    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d289">

                <pb facs="0506" n="220" xml:id="pg220"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.857Ea3</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gerard</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-04-01T16:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 1, 1916, 4 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2874. The Department is informed that on March 28 the British steamer <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Eagle Point</hi> with Americans on board was torpedoed
                    after surrender while bound from St. Johns, New Brunswick, to Cherbourg, and
                    that the persons on board were left in two small boats in a heavy sea with a
                    stormy wind blowing, 130 miles south of Queenstown. Please make immediate
                    inquiry of the German Government as to whether this vessel was torpedoed by a
                    submarine belonging to Germany or its allies. A prompt reply is expected.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
